Here a short discusstion is provided of how this project is related to FAIR data principles.

Starting at the Acronym F in FAIR, where F stand for "Findable" where gathered data is clearly stored under plots where we use standard metrics for showing the quality of the produced test of the neural network and the README gives a clear instruction list on what file does what and how to get started with using the provided coding tools. 
This project does however not give an DOI for the provided ATLAS data which makes it very hard to identify and test that our used data is true and has not been tampered with. This project was also originaly posted in a private Gitlab branch which would make it "difficult" for other to find it in the first place. 

For A, "Accessible", the data is easy to access as it is provided and others are free to use it since it shared under the "Creative Commons CC0 public domain dedication" making it not needed to use any authentication tools to use it.

By I, "Interoperable", the data and the python tools are easy to implement because of the small number of 4 common libraries used where all of them are installed by running requirements.txt which is explained by the README.md. The used data was given as as .CSV which is standard for this type of data.

R, "Reusable", is something the data is but it does pose some problems. How to use the code and when to run what file is clearly explained under the README.md and for detialed explinations behind the structure of the code is given in Project_documentation.md. This allows a user to easaly look up the general structure of how we got our plots and how to replicate them. 
However the usage license of the data was not initialy stated which makes it unattractive for them to use our tools to further their research.
